{"name":"Opaline","hex":"#c1d1c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"opaline","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Resembling opal in having a milky white iridescence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clear to white liquid secreted by sea hares (genus Aplysia) that becomes viscous upon contact with water.","Any of several minerals resembling opal, including a variety of yellow chalcedony."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/opaline"}],"entries":[{"title":"Opalinidae","blocks":[{"text":"The opalines are a small group of peculiar heterokonts, currently assigned to the family Opalinidae, in the order Slopalinida. Their name is derived from the opalescent appearance of these microscopic organisms when illuminated with full sunlight. Most opalines live in the large intestine and cloaca of anurans, though they are sometimes found in fish, reptiles, molluscs and insects; whether they are parasitic is not certain. The unusual features of the opalines, first observed by Antonie van Leeuwenhoek in 1683, has led to much debate regarding their phylogenetic position among the protists."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Parasite140015-fig2_Protoopalina_pingi_%28Opalinidae%29_Microscopy.tif/lossy-page1-330px-Parasite140015-fig2_Protoopalina_pingi_%28Opalinidae%29_Microscopy.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opalinidae"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Parasite140015-fig2_Protoopalina_pingi_%28Opalinidae%29_Microscopy.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Parasite140015-fig2_Protoopalina_pingi_%28Opalinidae%29_Microscopy.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Parasite140015-fig2_Protoopalina_pingi_%28Opalinidae%29_Microscopy.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Parasite140015-fig2_Protoopalina_pingi_%2